Q: What are ways to Draw Rectangle on Image? Image display. But Rectangle does not display. Canvas element JavaScript A: <p>The problem is timing: image.addEventListener(&quot;load&quot;, () =&gt; ctx.drawImage(image, 20, 20)) is asynchronous, the browser has to fetch Image.png first, so that callback runs later, after all your synchronous code (including the rectangle drawing) has already finished.</p>
<p>Your code actually runs in this order:</p>
<p>image.src starts loading,</p>
<p>the synchronous ctx.drawImage(image, 0, 0, 512, 512, 50, 40, 185, 185) runs immediately — but the image isn't loaded yet, so this draws nothing (or errors silently),</p>
<p>rectangle and green square get drawn,</p>
<p>and later, once the image finishes loading, the load event fires and draws the image right over your rectangle</p>
<p>Fix: put all your drawing code inside the load handler, so it only runs once the image is actually ready, and draw the rectangle after the image inside that same callback:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const canvas = document.querySelector(&quot;canvas&quot;);
const ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);

const width = (canvas.width = window.innerWidth);
const height = (canvas.height = window.innerHeight);

const image = new Image();
image.src = &quot;Image.png&quot;;

image.addEventListener(&quot;load&quot;, () =&gt; {
  //-------------------------------
  //draw image onto canvas element
  //-------------------------------
  ctx.drawImage(image, 20, 20);
  ctx.drawImage(image, 0, 0, 512, 512, 50, 40, 185, 185);

  //------------------------
  //draw rectangle on image
  //------------------------
  ctx.fillStyle = &quot;red&quot;;
  ctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 150);

  ctx.fillStyle = &quot;green&quot;;
  ctx.fillRect(75, 75, 100, 100);
});

canvas.setAttribute(&quot;aria-label&quot;, &quot;Image&quot;);
</code></pre>

Q: How to redirect from index.html to main.html after animation ends? A: <p>The main issue, <code>window.location.href</code> seems to get a wrong relative URL part. You should show a full URL in the question.</p>
<p>The other issue, you unlikely want to store the animation page in the navigation history, and you want to use <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Location/replace" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>window.location.replace()</code></a></p>
<pre><code>setTimeout(() =&gt; {
    window.location.replace(&quot;&lt;base-url&gt; + main.html&quot;);
}, 5000);
</code></pre>

Q: Build failure in flutter awesome_notifications due to android receiver A: <p>Documentation is misleading.</p>
<p>Replace this line:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code> &lt;receiver android:name=&quot;me.carda.awesome_notifications.core.broadcasters.receivers.RefreshSchedulesReceiver&quot; android:exported=&quot;true&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>With this and it <em>should</em> work fine:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>        &lt;receiver android:name=&quot;me.carda.awesome_notifications.DartRefreshSchedulesReceiver&quot; android:exported=&quot;true&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Edit:<br />
When I was facing this problem, I did a workaround. I was facing it only when targetSdk was set to above 34, so in debug mode I set targetSdk to 34, and in release mode I set it to whatever was desired for play store publishing, and surprisingly, it didn't crash in release mode more often, yes sometimes, very less often it did, but it was unnoticeable, because the app got closed and I opened again and then it didn't crash. Note that I did all this while keeping the manifest &lt;reciever&gt; as in the question, I mean this one `</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;receiver android:name=&quot;me.carda.awesome_notifications.core.broadcasters.receivers.RefreshSchedulesReceiver&quot; android:exported=&quot;true&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and not as in my above answer. hope that's clear.</p>
<p>And yes, there is something else we can do for notification schedules getting removed after a phone restart, so what we can do here is, using workmanager, we can run a periodic task that runs for let's say every 30 minutes or 1 hour or whatever interval you prefer, and it checks if schedules are removed or not, if removed(by an app restart), we can run our notification scheduling logic again. We can do that like this:</p>
<p>So, inside our workmanager callbackDispatcher, we can run a task for rescheduling notifications like so:</p>
<pre><code>if (task == WorkmanagerTasks.rescheduleTaskNotifs.taskName) {
// Check if scheduled list is empty or not, if empty, it means schedules were removed by an app restart
      final list = await AwesomeNotifications().listScheduledNotifications();
      if (list.isEmpty) {
        MiniLogger.d(&quot;Scheduled notifications list is empty, rescheduling task notifications...&quot;);
        final tasks = await getIt&lt;TaskRepository&gt;().getAllAsync();
        for (final task in tasks) {
          await getIt&lt;TaskNotificationController&gt;().createTaskNotifications(
            task,
          );
        }
      }
</code></pre>
<p>And in our app logic, we can schedule this task like so:</p>
<pre><code>await Workmanager().registerPeriodicTask(
    taskName,
    taskName,
    existingWorkPolicy: .replace,
    frequency: Duration(hours: 2),
  );
</code></pre>
